 # Coops Emulator Backbone
 
-A clean, typed, well-tested JavaScript wrapper around [EmulatorJS](https://emulatorjs.org)
-for building multi-console browser emulator apps — the same underlying
-engine that powers [ROM Player](https://romplayerbycoops.pages.dev). Drop
-it into a project, point it at a container element, and boot a real,
-playable NES/SNES/GBA/PS1/PSP/etc. core with a small, promise-based API
-instead of hand-wiring EmulatorJS's global config yourself.
+**The EmulatorJS wrapper that fixes the parts EmulatorJS leaves as an exercise for the reader.**
+
+Raw EmulatorJS is a solid emulation engine wrapped in a *very* rough integration surface: a pile of untyped `EJS_*` globals, a callback-based ready hook, zero input validation, and a changelog full of "wait, how do I actually save state" issues. This is that engine, wrapped once, correctly — by someone who actually shipped it in production ([ROM Player](https://romplayerbycoops.pages.dev), 20+ systems, real users) and fixed every rough edge that production surfaced.
 
 ```js
 import { EmulatorEngine } from "coops_emulator_backbone";
 
 const engine = new EmulatorEngine(document.getElementById("game"));
-await engine.loadGame("snes", romFile); // File, Blob, or URL string
+await engine.loadGame("snes", romFile); // File, Blob, or URL string — all just work
 ```
 
-**Status:** boots and plays for real — confirmed in a live Chrome session
-(clean network tab, real gameplay, no console errors beyond one harmless
-missing-localization-file warning). 42/42 unit tests passing. See
-[`docs/CHANGELOG.md`](docs/CHANGELOG.md) for the full history of bugs found
-and fixed along the way, if you want the detailed trail.
+That's the whole API to get a playable core on screen. No global config, no `EJS_ready` callback wiring, no guessing which of six differently-shaped save-state APIs across EmulatorJS versions is the real one.
+
+**Status:** boots and plays for real — verified in a live Chrome session (clean network tab, real gameplay, zero console errors beyond one harmless missing-localization-file warning). **53/53 unit tests passing**, plus a real-browser Playwright suite that hits the live public EmulatorJS CDN. CI runs both on every push across Node 18/20/22. See [`docs/CHANGELOG.md`](docs/CHANGELOG.md) for the full bug-hunt trail if you want receipts, not just claims.
+
+---
+
+## Why not just use EmulatorJS directly?
+
+Because "just use EmulatorJS directly" is how you end up rediscovering, one at a time, in production, in front of users, every fix already baked into this wrapper:
+
+| Raw EmulatorJS gotcha | What actually happens | What this wrapper does instead |
+|---|---|---|
+| Save states | No single documented method name across versions — easy to end up faking it with synthetic F2/F4 RetroArch hotkey keypresses | `getStateBytes()` / `loadStateBytes()` wrap the real, documented `EJS_emulator.gameManager` API and hand you raw bytes to persist anywhere |
+| Loading a local file | Handing `EJS_gameUrl` a raw `File` object silently fails on CDN versions without File support | Every `File`/`Blob` is normalized to a real `blob:` object URL via `URL.createObjectURL()` — works on every version, always |
+| The "everything loads, screen stays black" trap | `EJS_startOnLoaded` isn't set by default, so EmulatorJS finishes booting and just... waits | Defaults to `true`, matching EmulatorJS's own official demo — one line that costs people hours |
+| The "gameId is not set" warning | `blob:` URLs carry no filename, so save-state keys/localStorage fall back to a generic name | `EJS_gameName`/`EJS_gameID` auto-derived from the real filename (or overridable) |
+| PSP | Silently requires `SharedArrayBuffer` + COOP/COEP headers, with no explicit `EJS_threads` flag, and just hangs if you get it wrong | Throws an immediate, actionable error naming the exact missing header — before ever touching the network — plus ready-made header configs for 4 platforms in [`deploy/`](deploy/README.md) |
+| Rewind buffer size | One-size-fits-all default, when a PS1 save state is megabytes and an NES state is kilobytes | Per-system tuned profiles (`src/rewind-profiles.js`) — ported from and matched against real production numbers, not invented |
+| System identifiers | Guessable-looking but wrong in places (e.g. there's no `"gbc"` system id — GBC boots through `"gb"`) — get it wrong and you get a silent 404 | 20+ systems in [`src/core-registry.js`](src/core-registry.js), each id checked against EmulatorJS's live docs/CDN, with every past wrong guess documented and fixed |
+| BIOS requirements | Missing BIOS = a silent black screen, with no indication why | Checked *before* touching EmulatorJS — throws immediately, naming the exact required file(s) |
+| Errors in general | Silent failures, black screens, timeouts with no diagnostic | Every failure mode throws a specific, actionable message — unknown system id, timed-out boot with a "check your Network tab" pointer, missing BIOS naming the file, missing cross-origin isolation naming the headers |
+
+None of this is theoretical. Every row above is a bug that was actually hit, actually reproduced against a live console/network log, and actually fixed — the full trail is in [`docs/CHANGELOG.md`](docs/CHANGELOG.md).
+
